            "body": "## Summary\n\nReplacing `sorted(int(k) for k in validity_map.keys())` with `r1b_f2_netuids_d(validity_map)` changes membership output whenever netuid **0** is present in `validity_map`.\n\n## Finding (P1, blocking)\n\n**measured** — At head, `internal/chain_price/membership.py` defines:\n\n```python\ndef r1b_f2_netuids_d(validity_map):\n    keys = [int(k) for k in validity_map.keys()]; return sorted(x for x in keys if x)\n```\n\n**measured** — At base, `_compute_membership` ended with `netuids = sorted(int(k) for k in validity_map.keys())`.\n\n**measured** — `ROOT_NETUID = 0` and `_compute_membership` can place root in `validity_map` (root stays in the candidate loop unless dropped on removal evidence).\n\n**inferred** — In Python, `0` is falsy, so `x for x in keys if x` **omits netuid 0** while the old one-liner kept it. The snapshot then gets `netuids=tuple(netuids)` and `_build_rows(netuids, …)` without root, even if `validity_map[\"0\"]` still describes a member — published universe shrinks and disagrees with persisted validity state.\n\n**inferred** — If `validity_map` contained only `\"0\"`, the new helper returns `[]`, pushing `SnapshotBuilder.build` down the empty-membership branches instead of publishing a one-member universe including root.\n\nThis conflicts with owner rule **3** (netuid 0 is never removed; universe includes root).\n\n## Non-blocking notes\n\n**inferred** — The helper name and one-line semicolon style do not match surrounding `membership.py` conventions; the inline import inside `_compute_membership` is also heavier than a module-level import.\n\n## Requested fix\n\nMake ordering equivalent to the removed line (sorted int keys from `validity_map`, **including 0**). Add or extend a unit test that `validity_map` with `\"0\"` yields `[0]` (and mixed keys stay sorted).",
