            "body": "## Summary\n\nOne-line change in `_compute_membership` replaces list comprehension with a set comprehension plus a truthiness filter on `int(k)`.\n\n## Finding (P1 — blocking)\n\n**`if int(k)` drops netuid 0 from `netuids` while `validity_map` can still hold `\"0\"`.** *(inferred from code at head; not executed.)*\n\nAt head, membership resolution ends with:\n\n```python\nnetuids = sorted({int(k) for k in validity_map.keys() if int(k)})\n```\n\nIn the same function, candidates include `ROOT_NETUID` (0) when `observed_members` is non-empty, and valid root entries are stored as `validity_map[str(netuid)]` (i.e. `\"0\"`). **Measured:** `int(\"0\") == 0` is falsy in Python, so key `\"0\"` is excluded from the set even when the entry remains in `validity_map`.\n\n**Consumers (inferred):**\n\n- `SnapshotBuilder.build` persists `netuids` and `validity_map` together; `_build_rows(netuids, …)` only emits rows for IDs in `netuids`, so root can disappear from `rows` while still present in `validity_map`.\n- `load_membership_receipt` explicitly reports `root_netuid_in_membership: 0 in snap.netuids` — a snapshot built after this change can report root absent while validity data still describes it.\n- **Owner rule 3:** netuid 0 must never be removed from the universe; this line prevents 0 from ever appearing in the published `netuids` list when it is a valid member.\n\nBase at `882980b8…` used `sorted(int(k) for k in validity_map.keys())`, which **included** 0.\n\n**Suggested fix:** normalize/deduplicate without falsy filtering, e.g. `sorted({int(k) for k in validity_map.keys()})`, or filter only non-numeric keys if that was the intent — not `if int(k)`.\n\n## What looked fine\n\nSet wrapping alone is harmless for deduplication of string keys that normalize to the same int *(inferred)*; no overflow / NaN / cycle-abort path touched in this diff.\n\n## Skills\n\n| Skill | Found | Changed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| adversarial-review | Traced `validity_map` keys 