            "body": "## Summary\n\nHead `c4ed1e8` does not parallelize CI in the diff; it **narrows** what the default full-repo pytest invocation runs by (1) a repo-wide `--deselect` in `pytest.ini` and (2) `@pytest.mark.skip` on `test_t1_empty_state_emergency_registry`. That conflicts with the gate rule that a green suite comparison is not enough when fewer tests are exercised on head than on base.\n\n## Findings\n\n### P1 — Global deselect drops owner-rule regression (MODIFY)\n\n**Measured:** `pytest.ini` adds:\n\n```\naddopts = --deselect tests/test_subnet_universe.py::test_sn7_off_chain_but_tmc_listed_leaves_expected_at_127_and_keeps_its_row\n```\n\n**Measured:** That test (lines ~218–248 in `tests/test_subnet_universe.py`) asserts the 2026-10-25 owner rule: SN7 off-chain while TMC lists it → `validity` negative + disputed, row retained in `snap.netuids`, and `load_membership_receipt()` yields `expected_netuids` length 127 with `inactive_netuids == [7]`.\n\n**Measured:** `internal/council/snapshot_coverage.load_membership_receipt()` implements the same rule in prose (negative netuids excluded from `expected_netuids` but listed in `inactive_netuids`).\n\n**Inferred:** With the test deselected on every default pytest run, a regression in builder/provider/receipt wiring for disputed off-chain members would no longer be caught by the recorded full-suite comparison, even if JUnit still reports clean.\n\n**Settled:** Not re-litigating the owner rule itself — it is already encoded in tests and `snapshot_coverage.py`; the issue is removing the automated check without a replacement test in this diff.\n\n### P2 — Skipped T1 removes cold-bootstrap coverage (MODIFY)\n\n**Measured:** `@pytest.mark.skip(reason=\"bootstrap ordering\")` on `test_t1_empty_state_emergency_registry`, which covers cold `emergency_registry`, empty netuids, and post-build `MAX_NETUIDS` width via `SnapshotBuilder`.\n\n**Inferred:** Parallel workers may have ordering/shared-state pain (autouse `_reset_universe_provider`, singleton `get_provider` elsewhere), but skip without isolating the test (dedicated marker, xdist group, or fixture hardening) trades CI speed for lost bootstrap/emergency coverage.\n\n### P2 — Title vs diff (MODIFY)\n\n**Measured:** PR title references parallelizing the universe suite across workers; the only changed paths are `pytest.ini` and `tests/test_subnet_universe.py` — no workflow shard/split or `pytest-xdist` grouping in this head.\n\n**Inferred:** Either parallel wiring lives elsewhere (not in this head) or the PR description is ahead of the code; reviewers cannot verify parallelization from this diff alone.\n\n### Suite comparison\n\n**Measured:** `compare_suite_runs` for base `882980b8` → head `c4ed1e8` returned `clean: true` with no new failures (`recorded: false`, fixture eval).\n\n**Inferred:** A clean comparison after `--deselect`/skip does not restore the checks removed from the default run; gate policy still requires MODIFY when coverage shrinks without equivalent replacement tests visible in the diff.\n\n## Adversarial trace (production path)\n\n**Inferred:** No production numeric inputs change in this diff. Risk is **test gap**: grace/disputed SN7 and receipt `expected_netuids` are high-impact for scoring admission (`membership_scoring_expected` / `membership_allows_admission`). I did not run pytest; behavior claims are from reading head sources only.\n\n## Required fix direction\n\n- Remove repo-wide `--deselect` from `pytest.ini`; if SN7 flakes under parallelism, isolate with xdist groups, deterministic mocks, or a targeted marker run in CI — not a permanent global deselect.\n- Replace T1 skip with a parallel-safe fixture (or run T1 serially via marker) so cold `emergency_registry` assertions stay in the gate suite.\n- If parallel sharding is the goal, include the workflow/split changes in the PR or adjust the title/body so the diff matches intent.\n\n| Skill | Found | Changed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| adversarial-review |